[interviewer · INTRO] Your client is Brew & Bean, a specialty coffee chain with 200 locations across the US. Over the past two years, their net profit margin has declined from 24% to 6%, even though revenues have grown 15% over the same period. The CEO has hired us to identify the root cause of this profit decline and recommend how to reverse it. How would you approach this problem?

How would you like to approach this? Feel free to take a moment to gather your thoughts first.

[candidate silent 12s]

[candidate · 1:42] Okay, let me make sure I have the situation right: revenue is up 15% over two years, but net margin fell from 24% to 6%. So the problem is almost certainly on the cost side, since revenue is growing — though I'd still want to check whether revenue grew for the "wrong" reason, like discounting driving volume while prices fell.

My approach would be three buckets. First, revenue quality: price per transaction versus transaction volume, and mix across stores and products. Second, costs, split into COGS — coffee beans, milk, cups — and operating costs like labor, rent, utilities, plus corporate overhead. Third, external context: have the 200 locations changed, like new store openings dragging margin?

Can I start with the cost side? Do we have a breakdown of costs as a percent of revenue, two years ago versus today?
