[interviewer · STRUCTURE · 3:17] Okay. Now, you asked for the cost breakdown as a percent of revenue. Which cost line would you look at first, and why?

[candidate · 4:47] I'd look at COGS first — coffee beans and dairy specifically. Two reasons. One, for a specialty coffee chain COGS is usually one of the two biggest lines alongside labor, and a margin drop of 18 points is huge, so it most likely comes from a big line, not a small one. Two, commodity coffee prices are volatile and externally driven, so a two-year swing there is very plausible and the client wouldn't necessarily have noticed it eating margin if they held menu prices flat.

Labor would be my immediate second — wage inflation and minimum wage increases over two years are real.

So: can I see COGS and labor as a percent of revenue, then versus now? And if you have it, a split of COGS into beans, dairy, and packaging — that would tell me whether this is one input or broad inflation.
