[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 6:24] Your reconciliation holds. Other input costs — dairy, packaging, and food — are up 37.5% over the past two years. The bean share today isn't something I have, so which data would you pull to tell a bean problem from a broader input problem?

[candidate · 7:54] Let me actually test it with what I have. Index revenue at 100 in both periods so these are per-dollar-of-revenue figures. Two years ago COGS is 42, beans are 25% of that, so 10.5 beans and 31.5 other. Other inputs up 37.5% gives 31.5 × 1.375 = 43.3. Total COGS today is 58, so beans would be 58 − 43.3 = 14.7, versus 10.5 — about a 40% increase.

So beans and everything else rose at roughly the same rate. That says broad input inflation, not a bean-specific shock. I'll flag the caveat: that math holds per dollar of revenue, which assumes menu prices and mix didn't shift much.

Which is exactly what I'd want next — do we have menu price history over the two years? If prices were held flat while inputs rose ~38%, that's the real root cause.
