[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 7:57] Okay. I'll give you the menu price figure, and the bean price data as well, so you can check your inference directly. Menu prices have not changed in two years. Raw coffee bean costs are up 40% over the past two years.

[candidate · 9:27] That lands right on my estimate — 40% beans, 37.5% everything else. So it's broad input inflation, not a single commodity, and menu prices flat for two years means none of it was passed through. That's the root cause.

And it reframes the revenue growth: with prices flat, the 15% revenue growth is essentially all volume — more transactions, same ticket. So they've been selling more coffee at a worse margin each cup, which actually amplifies the problem rather than offsetting it.

Quick sizing on the fix: inputs are 58 points of revenue. To get back to roughly 24% margin, they need about 18 points. A flat price increase of X% raises revenue and leaves input dollars unchanged — so at +15% price, revenue is 115, costs stay 94, margin is 21/115, about 18%. So mid-to-high teens price increase gets most of it back, before any volume loss.

This is harder than I expected — but is volume elasticity something we have?
