[interviewer · RECOMMENDATION · 9:29] Understood. Elasticity isn't in the information I have. Beyond a price increase, what else could the client do to reverse the margin decline?

[candidate · 10:59] Fine, I'll assume we need to test elasticity rather than know it — specialty coffee tends to be fairly inelastic given the premium positioning, but I wouldn't bet 18 points of margin on that alone.

Other levers, roughly in order of impact. One, procurement: 200 locations is real scale, so longer-term fixed-price contracts or hedging on beans and dairy, and consolidating suppliers. That attacks the input cost directly instead of passing it on. Two, mix shift: push higher-margin items — food, specialty drinks, larger sizes — where the markup over input cost is better than drip coffee. Three, portion and waste control: if dairy is up 37.5%, waste on milk and unsold food is now expensive, so tightening it has more leverage than it used to. Four, overhead, the 2-point creep — smaller, but it's pure margin.

Damn, I keep wanting more data here. Do we know store-level margin spread, old versus new?
