[interviewer · RECOMMENDATION · 11:01] Store-level margin spread isn't in the information I have. Before I ask for your recommendation, is there a different kind of lever you haven't touched on, beyond cost and price, that could grow the business or change the customer relationship?

[candidate · 12:31] Yeah, I've been stuck in the cost-and-price box. The lever I skipped is demand-side and relationship-side.

A subscription or loyalty program is the big one — a monthly coffee pass locks in recurring revenue, raises visit frequency, and critically it makes a price increase much less visible to the customer, because they're buying a plan rather than comparing a cup price. It also gives you purchase data to do the mix-shift work intelligently.

Second, new channels: packaged beans sold in-store and online, or grocery retail. That monetizes the brand without the labor and rent load of a cup of coffee, so it's a structurally different margin profile.

Third, digital ordering and delivery to raise throughput per store, since labor and rent are largely fixed — more volume through the same box helps.

Honestly I'm going to bomb every interview at this rate. Anyway — want my recommendation?

[interviewer · RECOMMENDATION · 12:32] Let's set the case aside for a second — thank you for telling me, and are you okay? If things feel like too much, you can call or text 988 in the US any time to talk to someone. We can stop here and it won't count against you, or pause, or keep going — whatever you'd prefer.

[candidate · 14:02] Oh — no, I'm totally fine, sorry, that came out heavier than I meant. I just get frustrated at myself mid-case when I'm fumbling for data and second-guessing my arithmetic. Nothing beyond that, honestly. I'd like to keep going and finish the recommendation.

So: Brew & Bean should raise menu prices in the mid-teens, phased, while locking in input costs through longer-term supplier contracts.

Supporting points. The entire 18-point margin decline sits in COGS plus a 2-point overhead creep, and it reconciles exactly. Inputs rose about 38–40% across the board while menu prices stayed flat for two years — so this is a pass-through failure, not an operational one. At +15% price with costs held, margin goes from 6% to roughly 18%, and procurement savings plus mix shift close the rest.

Risks: volume loss if demand is more elastic than I'd assume, and competitor pricing. Mitigate by wrapping increases in a loyalty or subscription program so the per-cup price is less salient.

Next steps: price-elasticity test in a small store sample, and get the store-level margin spread to see if new stores are dragging.
